  "summary": "Oil drums hammered into chromatic orchestras — the sound of Carnival resistance.",
  "description": "Steelpan emerged in 1930s-40s Port of Spain when carnival percussion bans drove young men to tune bamboo (tamboo bamboo) and then discarded oil drums: sinking, grooving and hammering the barrel-head until it sang chromatic notes, arranged in families — tenor pans for melody, cello pans, six-bass units — into full panyards like Renegades, Desperadoes and All Stars. Pan competitions (Panorama) during Carnival made the instrument the soundtrack of national life; in 1992 Trinidad declared it the national instrument, and today steel orchestras tour world concert halls while panyards stay working-class academies where generations tune, arrange and perform.",
  "culturalContext": "Steelpan rose from carnival drum-bans and oil-drum scrap to Trinidad's national instrument (declared 1992): panyards are working-class academies where generations tune and arrange, Panorama competitions soundtrack Carnival, and the instrument carries Trinidad's story of resistance-turned-world-music to concert halls globally.",
